    "name":  "Prostate cancer",
    "module":  "oncology",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Prostate cancer is the most common cancer in men in Western countries and the second leading cause of male cancer deaths. It arises from the glandular epithelium of the prostate and is graded histologically by the Gleason score. Most early-stage prostate cancers are asymptomatic and detected via elevated PSA on screening. Locally advanced disease causes lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS) indistinguishable from BPH. Metastatic disease (most commonly to bones) causes bone pain, pathological fractures, and spinal cord compression. Hormone-sensitive (castration-sensitive) disease is treated with androgen deprivation therapy.",
